<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Movie Is... Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Interpret characters' motives in short stories. Reading: Identify the main ideas of persuasive texts and their arguments. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret characters' motives in short story descriptions by answering questions about key decisions and actions (e.g., "Why did the character move to another city?" "Because his/her family got a new job"). Can identify the main ideas of persuasive texts about books or movies (e.g., "What is the main message of this movie review?"). Can accurately identify and produce sounds in minimal pairs (e.g., distinguishes between /ʊ/ in "full" and /uː/ in "fool").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows three distinct movie posters or book covers on the board (e.g., an action movie, a documentary, a fantasy book). Students work in pairs to discuss: "What genre is this? Do you like this kind of movie/book? Why or why not?" They share their preferences using "I like watching action movies because they are exciting." or "I prefer reading fantasy books over documentaries because they are more engaging." (5 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models how to identify movie genres and express preferences. The teacher points to a poster and says, "This is an action movie. I like watching action movies because they are thrilling. I prefer action movies over comedies." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of words like "genre," "prefer," and "because," and ensures clear articulation of the /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ sounds in third-person singular verbs if applicable (e.g., "He *watches*," "She *loves*"). (3 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of genre and preference expressions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I like reading poetry,' what kind of book do I enjoy?" (Expected answer: "Poetry books.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you 'prefer' something, do you like it more or less?" (Expected answer: "More.")</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also quickly reviews vocabulary from Lesson 1 like 'character', 'plot', 'theme' by asking, "What is the 'plot' of a movie?" (Expected answer: "The story.") (2 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with a short, engaging movie review (approx. 100-120 words) for an animated film, perhaps titled "The Adventures of Luna." The review includes descriptions of the main <b>character</b>, parts of the <b>plot</b>, the film's <b>theme</b>, and expresses opinions using adjectives like <b>entertaining</b> and <b>emotional</b>. It also uses past simple to describe events and present simple for opinions. For example, "Luna was a brave character who *decided* to move to a new city..." and "I think this movie is very <b>engaging</b> because it has an amazing <b>plot</b>." (Genre: Movie Review, Register: Informal/Descriptive, Format: Short persuasive text).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Interpret characters' motives in short stories. Reading: Identify the main ideas of persuasive texts and their arguments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students first read the review silently to get the main idea (gist). (3 minutes)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What kind of movie is 'The Adventures of Luna' and what is the reviewer's general opinion?" (Students read quickly to answer.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements about specific details in the review, for example: "Luna was a shy character." or "The movie's theme is about friendship." (Students re-read to find specific information.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match key vocabulary words (e.g., 'character', 'plot', 'theme') from a list to their definitions or examples provided in the text.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher monitors, assists with vocabulary, and guides students to underline sentences that express main ideas or character descriptions. (7 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for character motives and main ideas; Present simple for preferences/reasons, Past simple for events, Verb + -ing + noun + because; Vocabulary: character, plot, theme, entertaining, engaging, emotional; Pronunciation: minimal pairs /ʊ/ and /uː/, third-person singular -s/-es/-ies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Character Motives:</b> Students re-read specific sections of "The Adventures of Luna" review. The teacher provides questions about the character's actions and motives: "Why did Luna decide to move to a new city?" or "What made Luna feel emotional at the end?" Students write short answers based on the text, focusing on identifying direct or inferred reasons, using "because..." (e.g., "Luna decided to move because her family got a new job."). (5 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Main Ideas & Persuasion:</b> Students identify the main persuasive argument of the movie review. The teacher provides sentence stems: "The main message of this movie review is that 'The Adventures of Luna' is a __________ movie because __________." or "The reviewer wants people to __________ because __________." Students complete these sentences individually. (5 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness & Pronunciation Practice:</b> The teacher highlights words from the review or related vocabulary that contain the minimal pair sounds /ʊ/ (as in "full") and /uː/ (as in "fool"). For example: 'book' (/bʊk/) vs. 'pool' (/puːl/, related to movie/swimming). Students listen as the teacher says pairs of words and raise their hand for the /uː/ sound. Then, they practice reading sentences from the review that contain third-person singular verbs ending in "-s," "-es," and "-ies" (e.g., "The character *runs*," "The director *watches*," "It *finishes*") aloud in pairs, focusing on the correct /s/, /z/, or /ɪz/ pronunciation. (3 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Pair Check & Feedback:</b> Students exchange their answers from activity 1 and 2 with a partner. They read each other's responses and discuss if the interpretations of motives and main ideas are clear and supported by the text. They practice providing constructive feedback: "I think your answer for Luna's motive is good, but you could add..." or "I agree with your main message, it clearly explains why the reviewer thinks the movie is engaging." (2 minutes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Response. Students will work in small groups to read a new, short descriptive text about a different book or movie character and their initial situation. They will then interpret the character's likely motives and identify a possible main theme or argument implied by the description.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into groups of three. Each group receives a different short paragraph (around 70-80 words) describing a new book/movie character, their background, and a key event. For example, "Marco was a talented musician who loved playing his guitar. One day, his favorite guitar broke, and he felt very sad. His friend, Sofia, suggested he join a new band to *express* his feelings, even without his old guitar."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read their paragraph carefully, underlining key words related to character actions and emotions (verbs like 'loved', 'broke', 'felt', 'suggested', 'express'). (3 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In their groups, students discuss and collaboratively answer two questions based on their reading:
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why do you think Marco might agree to join a new band even though his guitar broke? What is his motive?" (They should use "because..." in their answer, e.g., "He might agree because he wants to continue to express his creativity and emotional side.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you think is the main message or theme this short story excerpt is trying to convey about challenges?" (e.g., "The main theme could be about finding new ways to enjoy and express yourself after a setback.")</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group writes down their agreed-upon answers on a small poster or a shared digital document. They must use target vocabulary (character, express, emotional, theme) and grammar (present simple, verb + -ing + noun + because). (7 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, one student from each group briefly presents their interpretation of the character's motive and the main theme to the class. They also take questions from other groups, explaining their interpretations. (5 minutes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentation and peer assessment of written responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will perform by orally presenting their group's interpretation of character motives and main ideas, and produce a written response with their group. Peers will evaluate the clarity and justification of the interpretations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can interpret character's motives (answers "Why?" effectively).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the main idea/theme of the short text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses present simple and "because" correctly for reasons.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporates target vocabulary (e.g., character, express, emotional, theme).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates effectively in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually before leaving the class:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about interpreting a character's motives today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to identify the main idea because..."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about reading a short movie review and understanding why a character does something?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading and discussing today did I enjoy the most, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading tasks successfully engaged students in interpreting character motives and identifying main ideas. The integration of phonemic awareness practice was brief but effective. Some students might need more scaffolding in identifying implicit motives. The group activity encouraged collaborative reading and discussion. For next lesson (Speaking), I will build on these interpretations and focus on active discussion of opinions and preferences about books and movies, possibly through role-playing a talk show where students discuss their favorite narratives and characters. (5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>